{"cuisine/huaiyang-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/huaiyang-cuisine","n":"Huaiyang cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Jiangsu cuisine","cuisine/jiangsu-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in China","intro":[["Huaiyang or Jianghuai cuisine is one of the Four Great Traditions in Chinese cuisine. It is derived from the native cooking styles of the region surrounding the lower reaches of the Huai and Yangtze rivers and centered on the cities of Huai'an, Yangzhou and Zhenjiang in Jiangsu Province. Although it is one of several sub-regional styles within Jiangsu cuisine, Huaiyang cuisine is widely seen in Chinese culinary circles as the most popular and prestigious style of Jiangsu cuisine, to a point where it is considered to be one of the Four Great Traditions that dominate the culinary heritage of China, along with Cantonese cuisine, Shandong cuisine, and Sichuan cuisine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"huaiyang-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huaiyang_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Dazhu gansi","dish/dazhu-gansi"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Jiangsu cuisine","cuisine/jiangsu-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Land of Fish and Rice: Recipes from the Culinary Heart of China","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Fuchsia Dunlop, 2016","note":"Jiangnan cookbook that includes Yangzhou and Huaiyang dishes.","url":"https://wwnorton.com/books/Land-of-Fish-and-Rice/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Jiangsu cuisine","cuisine/jiangsu-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/e/e6/Braised_Shredded_Chicken_with_Ham_and_Dried_Tofu_2011-04.JPG/500px-Braised_Shredded_Chicken_with_Ham_and_Dried_Tofu_2011-04.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"猫猫的日记本","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Braised_Shredded_Chicken_with_Ham_and_Dried_Tofu_2011-04.JPG","of":"Dazhu gansi"}},"cuisine/history-of-chinese-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/history-of-chinese-cuisine","n":"Historical Chinese cuisine","c":"#a99fb5","crumb":[["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Historical cuisine","intro":[["The history of Chinese cuisine is marked by both variety and change. The archaeologist and scholar Kwang-chih Chang says \"Chinese people are especially preoccupied with food\" and \"food is at the center of, or at least it accompanies or symbolizes, many social interactions\". Over the course of history, he says, \"continuity vastly outweighs change.\" He explains basic organizing principles which go back to earliest times and give a continuity to the food tradition, principally that a normal meal is made up of a plant based products consisting of grains, starch vegetables (菜; cài) and/or fish (鱼; yú) based dishes with very little red meat (红肉; hóngròu) consumption."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Chinese_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Recipes from the Garden of Contentment: Yuan Mei's Manual of Gastronomy","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Yuan Mei, translated by Sean J.S. Chen, 2019","note":"Annotated bilingual translation of the 18th-century Qing dynasty gastronomy classic Suiyuan Shidan, from Berkshire Publishing.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781614720430","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781614720430"}]}],"cont":null},"ingredient/pork":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/pork","n":"Pork","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 67 dishes across 62 cuisines","intro":[["Pork is the culinary name for the meat of the pig (Sus domesticus). It is the second most commonly consumed type of meat worldwide, following poultry, with evidence of pig husbandry dating back to 8000–9000 BCE."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{